WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:02.550 align:start position:0%
 
every day of this shoot  we're making something new and special  jordan peele takes a completely  different approach on the set of his  newest horror movie  and something that is a testament to why  i love movies and i'll be talking about  these days we made this movie forever  jordan peele's nope gives us a fresh  take on the classic genres of horror and  sci-fi with his iconic writing and  directing an incredible imax budget and  a legendary cinematographer nope brings  a deep thought-provoking message to life  with stellar visuals and captivating  performances to match  number one we're here for whatever  vision he wants to put on the screen  jordan peele's vision for this film was  to make it like an introduction to  horror for people who wouldn't typically  watch it like an open invitation this  movie will be scary but at the very  least i'm bringing you in the title nope  signifies the group of people who would  never in a million years be in a  situation that the film represents let  alone watch it on screen there's certain  things that are just going to make us go  nope it's done number two the complexity  behind it is more natural than you'd  think jordan peele's directing style is  actually pretty collaborative when it  comes to creating the characters the  most impressive thing about jordan is  that he allows the story to kind of find  itself the actors found their  character's intentions changing minute  by minute the more the story would  unfold to kind of be welcomed into a  very collaborative environment like that  is a huge blessing number three  speaking of building characters jordan  actually did let me improv a lot which  is really cool the great thing about  kiki palmer's character emerald is that  she was written in such a natural way  that her energy and comedic talent  popped off the screen what was written  of the character emerald was uh so much  what i like to play with in terms of my  comedic wheelhouse when jordan gave kiki  the script it was clear to her that he'd  already had her in mind when writing it  number four  jordan peele's impact on the horror  genre is legendary and i appreciate what  he does for our culture across the board  and just giving us these beautiful  pieces of representation kiki palmer has  played so many characters throughout her  career but with emerald's natural hair  and shamelessly real demeanor she turned  out to be one of the most meaningful for  her to play the kind of chick i get to  play you know it's the kind of character  that i feel like usually only guys get  to play number five an emotional scene  takes a comedic turn one of the great  things about working with horses is that  they don't really care if you're in the  middle of a scene if they have to go  they're going it's like a really  emotional scene i'm letting that lucky  go and then lucky just decided to do a  piss  the irony is not lost here and it was a  really long piece and i just had to wait  there  number six a simple farm town that holds  a lot of meaning to the film's  characters the amount of work and detail  and effort that went into constructing  that town is pretty insane jup's theme  park jupiter's claim was built in the  aguadusa desert which is a real place in  los angeles county as far as what it  represents in the film dupe is held  metaphorically prisoner even though he  built it all for himself this is his  nightmare this is his fever dream this  is his prison in every detail there are  hidden pockets of darkness the truth is  a lot simpler and darker than we could  ever imagine number seven especially for  a 65 million dollar imax movie jordan  peele describes this movie as a standout  from his other films i tried to write a  script that i didn't know how to pull it  off and then assemble the team to help  me pull it off as we mentioned before  jordan is a huge collaborator when it  comes to his filmmaking he wants  everyone to have a voice  every single department is firing off  these huge big swings  it was important for him to create an  environment where every person involved  is able to speak up and put forth their  own creative imprint we're all important  individually and in combination for all  of us is hugely important number eight  just like emerald jordan peel wrote oj  with daniel kaluuya in mind in an  interview with empire he describes his  character as a humble man destined for  an epic and otherworldly confrontation  he also speaks on why daniel was the  perfect person to play the role daniel's  craft is on a different level but it's  his warmth that grounds audiences even  during points of absolute madness  you may be watching a nightmare but when  he's on screen at least you've got your  brother with you and that's all you need  number nine it's very subtle but it's  there the underlying message for the  film all started when one of the  producers gave jordan a book on edward  muybridge godfather of motion picture  for you know some of these experimental  photography  that he took this was the first door  that opened up the exploration of the  film industry and all of the  exploitation that has always been part  of it that became an underlying theme  that is i think woven throughout the the  film not only that but there are several  other important underlying themes  throughout the movie let us know in the  comments which ones jumped out at you  number 10 we're talking consultations  with actual jellyfish experts jordan  peele worked with john dibiri an  aeronautics engineer and the centennial  chair professor at caltech as well as  the director of the biological  propulsion laboratory so yeah he  definitely knows a thing or two about  creating a massive ufo alien jellyfish  that jellyfish known as jean jacket  utilizes features of real marine life  throughout the film tibiary spoke with  thrillists about his inspirations it has  all of these interesting properties  about looking at it in the eye that  metamorphosis that it has in the end  going from more conventional ufo flying  saucer shape and at the end revealing  itself  number eleven though gordy the chimp  story is painfully tragic it's also  based on the real life story of a chimp  named travis we won't get into it here  but the story of gordy shares a major  lesson on the treatment of animals for  spectacle though in this film the chimp  was portrayed by actor terry notary who  is no stranger to this type of role he  also played rocket in planet of the apes  number 12 the film opens up with a major  cinema fact the very first assembly of  photographs to create a motion picture  was a two second clip of a black man on  a horse the only fictional thing about  it in the movie is his identity as  emerald's great great great grandfather  great there's another great grandfather  but in real life his true identity is no  longer known  number 13 but originally he wasn't  actually supposed to in an interview  with scifi brandon perea who plays angel  shared that his character not only does  it make it through the first draft but  also the second jordan was definitely in  a mode of let's wreck these people and  i'm like no man don't wreck me though  after talking to some of the studio  heads they all came to the consensus  that they needed angel until the very  end number 14. also known as the  alternate title apparently jordan peele  only thought of calling the movie nope  well into the pre-production stage of  the film but the title he was writing  with was little green men at the end of  the day the film felt singular and nope  was the only thing that i could i could  say to describe it because the film is  about among several other things greed  the little green men represent the men  on our dollar bills number fifteen we're  not promising any ufos but we have to  mention the fact that universal studios  reconstructed the park as part of its  studio tour on the hollywood backlot  universal even built an entire website  and social media profiles before the  movie premiered all of which were  entirely fictional but if you saw before  the film you'd think it was a totally  real theme park considering one of the  film's major themes is about spectacle  again the irony is not lost on us  so after going through all the  inspirations character collaborations  mishaps and the true stories behind  another one of jordan peel's brilliant  creations we're honestly just excited  for what he's gonna do next what are  some of your favorite moments from the  film

